# NWP Infrastructure .gitignore
# This file ignores everything except the core NWP infrastructure files
# Users will have their own nwp.yml configuration (ignored)
# and their own site installations (ignored)

# Ignore everything by default
/*
!/.gitattributes

# Whitelist NWP infrastructure files

# Sites directory (where site installations go)
# The folder is tracked but contents are ignored
!sites/
sites/*
!sites/.gitkeep

# sites/tmp/ is forbidden — see CLAUDE.md ("sites/tmp/ — NEVER CREATE").
# Explicit deny so even an accidental whitelist later in this file can't undo it.
sites/tmp/
sites/tmp/**

# /private/ holds operator-private handoff packs (Coder/Greg setup script,
# handbook, credentials). Defense in depth — explicit deny so even if a
# later whitelist accidentally re-includes it, this stays ignored.
# Same shape as the sites/ block above: the FOLDER is walkable, its contents
# are ignored. `private/` (a directory rule) would stop git descending at all
# and make the negations below unreachable; `private/*/` keeps every
# subdirectory ignored no matter what is whitelisted after it.
!private/
private/*
private/*/
# …with two VALUE-FREE exceptions. The token lifecycle's source of record and
# its audit trail had no history and no replication at all, which is a poor
# property for the thing that is supposed to be authoritative. These carry
# metadata only — dates, ids, and which code reads which key — never a value.
!private/token-consumers.md
!private/rotation-*.md
# NOTE (item 1, secrets-truth-and-gate): private/secrets-registry.yml stays
# ignored HERE, permanently, and that is now a measured decision rather than a
# deferral. The leakage gate (which has since landed) reports 162 findings over
# the registry: 66 live-domain-apex, 65 live-internal-domain, 25
# internal-bare-hostname, 4 operator-public-ip, 2 operator-personal-email — and
# ZERO credential findings. So the registry is value-free exactly as designed,
# and is simultaneously a complete map of the estate plus the operator's public
# IP and personal address. nwp/nwp is the public-release track and carries those
# three rules specifically to keep that material out of it; committing the
# registry here would be the leakage gate's own counterexample.
#
# The GOAL (history, review, a second copy) still holds and is now enforced:
# `pl secrets lint` fails with UNTRACKED-REGISTRY / UNCOMMITTED-REGISTRY /
# NO-REGISTRY-REMOTE, and `pl secrets registry-track` satisfies it by making
# private/ its own repo — which this rule keeps invisible to the outer one.
# Do not "fix" the lint error by adding `!private/secrets-registry.yml` here.

# Command entry points
# Users run ./setup.sh to bootstrap
# After setup, use the `pl` CLI command to run all other scripts
# (F23: legacy podcast.sh / install.sh root symlinks were removed; commands
#  live in scripts/commands/ and dispatch through `pl`.)
!setup.sh

# CLI completion (installed by setup)
!pl
!pl-completion.bash

# CI/CD and code quality
!.gitlab-ci.yml
!.github/
!.github/**
!.gitlab/
!.gitlab/**
!.hooks/
!.hooks/**
!renovate.json
!phpstan.neon

# Scripts directory
!scripts/
!scripts/**

# Rollback registry: per-host runtime data (snapshot paths point at
# specific remote files in the operator's home dir on the live host).
# Not portable, not for the repo. MUST come after the `!scripts/**`
# whitelist above so it actually wins.
scripts/commands/.rollback/
scripts/commands/.rollback/**

# Verification tracking
# .verification.yml is intentionally tracked: it documents which commands have
# been verified (real run vs. syntax-only) so badges and external readers can
# see what's actually exercised. It churns on every `pl verify` run, which is
# the price of that transparency.
!.verification.yml
!.badges.json
!.verification-scenarios/
!.verification-scenarios/**

# CI stats (F36 §6.5) — bootstrap config + readme tracked on main;
# runtime *.tsv samples live on the `stats` branch only (deny rule
# at the bottom of this file).
!.ci-stats/
!.ci-stats/README.md
!.ci-stats/bootstrap.yml

# Documentation and metadata
!README.md
!CHANGELOG.md
!CONTRIBUTING.md
!KNOWN_ISSUES.md
!LICENSE
!LICENSE-CC0
!LICENSE-MIT
!NOTICE
!CLAUDE.md

# Configuration templates (not user configs)
!example.nwp.yml
!.secrets.example.yml
!.secrets.data.example.yml
!.nwp-developer.example.yml

# THE CI-READABLE PROJECTION OF THE REVIEW MODE MUST BE TRACKED.
#
# The review mode is DERIVED from `approvers:` in private/secrets-registry.yml,
# which is a separate repo that CI cannot read. .nwp-review-mode is the generated
# projection CI reads instead, so it has to be tracked. `.gitignore` denies
# everything at the root (`/*`) and allowlists back, so it was silently ignored on
# first writing — present on the workstation, ABSENT in CI.
#
# Worth recording which way that failed: an unreadable mode resolves to `team`, the
# stricter one, so the symptom would have been CI holding every sensitive-path MR
# while `pl mr review-mode` said solo locally. Annoying, and visible. Had it
# defaulted to `solo`, the same bug would have silently disabled two-person review
# in the pipeline with nothing complaining. Fail-closed picked the direction where a
# mistake costs friction, not protection.
!.nwp-review-mode

# Pair contracts (ADR-0031 / ops#75): the template + the committable per-pair
# contracts (versions + public URLs only — no secrets). Deployed-version + RAG
# STATE lives in private/pairs/ which the private/ deny rule above keeps out.
!pair-contract.example.yml
!pairs/
!pairs/**

# Intersite data contracts (P74): JSON-Schema data contracts + validate.py +
# compat.py (backward-compat differ) + SHA256SUMS. Public shapes, no secrets.
# Whitelist the whole dir so future contract files aren't silently dropped by
# the root /* rule (the reason ops#96 compat.py went missing the first time).
!contracts/
!contracts/**

# Site class declarations (ADR-0036 / ops#153): the closed class registry plus
# one declaration per site, saying what that site IS and therefore which data
# invariants bind it. These MUST be tracked — that is the whole design. nwp.yml
# is never committed and sites/* is ignored above, so neither can carry a claim
# that switches the Art.9 consent gate off; such a claim has to be visible in a
# merge request. Metadata only: classes, postures, and attested row/member
# COUNTS — never a credential, never a row of member data.
# Whitelist the whole dir so a future declaration is not silently dropped by
# the root /* rule (the reason ops#96 compat.py went missing the first time).
!classes/
!classes/**

# Migration scripts (access via pl or scripts/commands/)

# Documentation directory
!docs/
!docs/**

# Libraries
!lib/
!lib/**

# nwp-server build-target manifests (ADR-0022/0024). The assembled artifact
# (build/out/) is a build product and stays ignored; only the manifests track.
!build/
build/*
!build/nwp-server.include
!build/nwp-server.deny-symbols
!build/nwp-solo.include

# Tests
!tests/
!tests/**

# Templates for new sites
!templates/
!templates/**

# F23 Phase 8: per-server infrastructure (email/, linode/, git/) now lives
# under servers/<name>/ — see the servers/ block below.
# F23 Phase 3: Mass Times scraper now lives in sites/mt/pipeline/.
# F23 Phase 2: Drupal custom modules now live in sites/<name>/web/modules/custom/.
# Pipelines and modules are intentionally untracked by the parent NWP repo
# (see the /sites/* rule above).

# Servers directory (per-host operator config).
#
# WAS: a blanket `servers/*`. That meant host configuration was versioned only
# when someone remembered `git add -f` — the 26 tracked files under servers/ all
# got in by force-add on 2026-07-25, and any NEW vhost, cron, systemd unit or
# php.ini override was silently invisible. Load-bearing artifacts (the fleet
# backup producer, the CVE-response upgrade script, the DR pull cron) therefore
# lived only on boxes, which is precisely the nwp-daily-audit lesson.
#
# NOW: an ALLOWLIST. Declared, reviewable service directories are tracked;
# identity, secrets and key material stay ignored. Adding a new service
# directory is a deliberate one-line change here, which is the point.
#
# Guarded by tests/unit/test-host.bats ("the generic mechanism the engine still
# ships is not git-ignored", "the server IDENTITY file and secrets stay ignored",
# and "per-host identity state is NOT engine-tracked" — see the split below).
!servers/
servers/*
!servers/.gitkeep
!servers/example-*.yml

# Tracked service directories (recurse into them).
!servers/*/
!servers/*/nginx/**
!servers/*/demo/**
!servers/*/linode/**
!servers/*/backup/**
!servers/*/email/**
!servers/*/system/**
# DECLARED php state (item 7). Distinct from system/php/**, which is CAPTURED:
# this is the intent we mean to apply, that is the reading of what is running.
# Keeping them in separate directories is what makes `diff` meaningful.
!servers/*/php/**

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ops#326 Phase 1 tranche 3 — THE SPLIT.
#
# The 2026-07-25 lesson above is intact: this state must be VERSIONED. What
# changed is WHICH repository versions it. nwp/nwp is the generic engine and is
# publicly mirrored (ops#101), and the state below is instance identity: real
# vhosts with real domains and real private site names, the operator crontab,
# mail aliases, ufw rules, authorized keys, host inventories. It is now tracked
# in a PRIVATE PER-SERVER REPOSITORY, in place — servers/<host>/.git, remote
# nwp/server-<host>. The files do not move, so every `pl` verb that reads
# servers/<host>/… keeps reading exactly the same path; only the repo boundary
# moves, and reviewability moves with it (the overlay repo has its own MRs).
#
# The guard changes shape accordingly: tests/unit/test-host.bats no longer asks
# "is this trackable by the engine?" but "is servers/<host>/ a repo with a
# remote and nothing unpushed?" (host_check_server_repos, wired into pl doctor).
#
# What the ENGINE still ships below is generic mechanism only.
servers/*/nginx/conf.d/**
servers/*/nginx/retired-*/**
servers/*/nginx/README.md
servers/*/system/**
servers/*/php/**
servers/*/postfix/**
servers/*/letsencrypt/**
servers/*/README.md

# ...generic mechanism the engine keeps: the GitLab-bundled-nginx renew hook and
# the deny-files snippet are product code, carry no instance identity, and are
# pinned by tests/unit/test-nginx-versioning.bats.
!servers/*/nginx/renew-hook.sh
!servers/*/nginx/snippets/
!servers/*/nginx/snippets/**
# The agent-host session supervisor units are shipped product, pinned by
# tests/unit/test-session-supervisor.bats.
!servers/*/system/systemd-nwp-session-supervisor.service
!servers/*/system/systemd-nwp-session-supervisor.timer
# DEFERRED, not kept: the fleet backup producer is being changed concurrently
# under ops#332. It moves to the per-server repo in the next tranche.
!servers/*/backup/nwp-box-backup.sh

# ...but NEVER the identity file, secrets, or any key/cert material, wherever
# under servers/ they appear. These re-ignores come last so they win.
servers/*/.nwp-server.yml
servers/**/.secrets*
servers/**/*.key
servers/**/*.pem
servers/**/id_rsa*
servers/**/id_ed25519*
servers/**/*.env

# SSH keys directory (structure tracked, contents ignored)
!keys/
!keys/.gitkeep

# Keep .gitignore itself
!.gitignore

# P61 leakage-hygiene gate files (authored as part of P61 + F32 Phase A)
!.gitleaks.toml
!.gitleaksignore
!.pre-commit-config.yaml

# P62 documentation-truth gate baseline (pl doc-truth; ops#53)
!.doc-truth-baseline

# CI-gate baselines (item 5, ci-gate-honesty). Same contract as
# .doc-truth-baseline / .gitleaksignore: SHRINK-ONLY records of pre-existing
# findings, so the gate is enforceable on day one and can only get stricter.
!.yq-first-baseline
!.security-meta-baseline

# Gate-honesty baselines (2026-08-02). A check that has never been proven to
# fail is not a check: .gate-redproof-baseline records every CI gate that has
# never been observed RED, .test-honesty-baseline records checks that assert
# less than they appear to. Both SHRINK-ONLY, both must be tracked or the gates
# cannot tell "nothing to report" from "nobody recorded anything".
!.gate-redproof-baseline
!.test-honesty-baseline

# Private-site-name baseline (ops#326). SHRINK-ONLY name-free path→count rows;
# the deny-list itself is NEVER tracked (private/site-names.deny + the
# NWP_SITE_NAME_DENYLIST CI variable). Whitelisted here explicitly because the
# root `/*` rule would otherwise swallow it silently — the exact trap
# .nwp-review-mode nearly fell into (see line ~126).
!.site-name-baseline

# bin/ holds operational helper scripts (audit-authors, migrate-* per F33, etc.)
# tracked alongside lib/ and scripts/
!bin/
!bin/**

# However, always ignore secrets and local overrides anywhere in the tree
# SSH key contents (keep directory but ignore all keys)
keys/*
!keys/.gitkeep
# Public keys only — the deploy-gate authorization list (ADR-0028, ops#79).
# Safe and intended to be distributed in-repo; contains no private material.
!keys/allowed_signers

# Secrets files - NEVER commit these
**/.secrets.yml
**/.secrets.yaml
**/.secrets.data.yml
**/.secrets.data.yaml
**/nwp.yml
**/.env.local
**/.ddev/config.local.yaml
**/.ddev/config.*.yaml
!**/.ddev/config.yaml
**/*.local.yml
**/*.local.yaml

# Generated output directories
scripts/commands/podcast-setup-*/

# CI stats runtime samples (F36 §6.5). Bootstrap config is checked in;
# *.tsv sample files live on the `stats` branch only.
.ci-stats/*.tsv

# Agent loop runtime state (P-agent-loop)
# The driver and helpers are tracked under scripts/agent-loop/; their state
# files and the kill switch are local-only.
.loop-paused
.agent-loop.state.json
/logs/
/logs/**

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Point-in-time security audit snapshots — private, not committed (findings + hostnames)
docs/security/audit-2026-05/

# ver provisioning kit: operator-verified tool pins (local trust anchors; see
# scripts/ver-provision/ver-kit.pins.example) and the assembled kit output
scripts/ver-provision/ver-kit.pins

# Pair runtime state — NOT source.
#
# `pl pair-smoke` and `pl rag` write per-site logs and RAG state under
# private/pairs/. On 2026-08-03 a `git add -A` in a worktree swept two of them
# into MR !354 and they became TRACKED on main: private/pairs/ssd.log (a
# smoke-run log) and private/pairs/ssd.live.rag (RAG state). The next `git pull`
# on the workstation then ABORTED — "untracked working tree files would be
# overwritten" — because the same files existed locally as runtime output. A
# committed log file is a merge conflict waiting for every host that runs the
# verb.
#
# private/ is allowlisted for a few genuine records (token-consumers, rotation
# logs), which is why these slipped through. Runtime state is excluded
# explicitly.
private/pairs/*.log
private/pairs/*.rag
private/pairs/*.cv

# ops#326: the private OVERLAY repo's content is never this repo's business.
# The unanchored `!classes/` / `!pairs/` / `!.gitignore` negations above exist
# for the SHIPPED top-level dirs, but they also re-include the overlay's
# private/classes/, private/pairs/ and its own .gitignore — which then show up
# as untracked in every engine status (the exact `git add -A` sweep hazard the
# note above records). Last-match-wins: re-ignore the overlay's shapes here.
private/classes/
private/pairs/
private/sanitizers/
private/agent-loop/
private/.gitignore

# ops#319 F4: the repo's Claude Code harness config ships WITH the repo and is
# reviewed like code (the PreToolUse raw-remote gate is registered there). Only
# settings.json is tracked; settings.local.json and anything else under
# .claude/ stays ignored. Explicit whitelist because the root `/*` deny above
# would otherwise swallow it silently — the exact trap .nwp-review-mode nearly
# shipped with (present locally, absent in CI).
!/.claude/
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json

# ops#319 F4 override ledger — where NWP_RAW_REMOTE_OK reasons land. Private
# operational record (commands may embed hostnames), never committed; the
# private/ allowlist above must not be widened to sweep it in.
private/raw-remote-overrides.log
